<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yu Deuk-gong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a>–<a href="/years/1807">1807</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yu Deuk-gong was a Korean scholar of the Silhak school in the late Joseon period, who served in the royal library under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeongjo-of-joseon">King Jeongjo</a>. In his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balhaego">Balhaego</a>, a study of the history of Balhae completed in 1784, he argued that Balhae and Unified Silla formed a period of Northern and Southern States, so that Balhae belonged to Korean history, a view widely held by Korean historians today.</p></div>
